# Which Hermes Agent engine versions this WebUI has actually been tested against.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# bootstrap.py used to install the engine with `curl -fsSL <installer> | bash`,
# which means "whatever shipped this morning". Upstream ships on the order of
# 5,000 commits per minor release, so two customers who onboard a week apart
# were getting materially different engines and nothing recorded which one.
#
# This is the same discipline `amelias-agent-ios` already uses with its
# UPSTREAM_TESTED_SHA / UPSTREAM_TRIAGED_SHA files: name the version you
# actually ran, and make anything outside that range a deliberate decision
# instead of an accident.
#
# How it is enforced
# ------------------
# `scripts/check_engine_contract.py --mode version` reads this file, reads the
# engine's own `hermes_cli/__init__.py:__version__`, and refuses a version
# outside the range. bootstrap.py calls it before starting the server.
# Set HERMES_WEBUI_ALLOW_UNTESTED_ENGINE=1 to proceed anyway — the point is
# that you had to say so.
#
# How to widen it
# ---------------
# Install the new engine, run `python3 scripts/check_engine_contract.py`
# (symbol contract) and the WebUI test suite against it, then move `max` and
# add a line to the log below. Do not widen it because a customer complained
# that the gate fired; the gate firing is the feature.

min = 0.20.5
max = 0.20.6

# Upstream's git tags are date-stamped, not semver — 0.20.6 ships as v2026.8.27 —
# so the tag cannot be derived from the version and has to be recorded next to it.
# CI clones `max_ref` and runs the symbol contract against it, which is what makes
# the gate meaningful on a machine that has no engine installed.
max_ref = v2026.8.27

